JaCoCo is a Java code-coverage tool.
Think of it as a measurement tool that watches your Java code while tests are running and tells you:

"Which parts of the code were actually executed by the tests?"


<plugin>
    <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
    <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>0.8.13</version>

    <executions>
        <execution>
            <goals>
                <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>

        <execution>
            <id>report</id>
            <phase>test</phase>
            <goals>
                <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>














For your Java + Maven + TestNG project, the easiest way is to add the JaCoCo Maven plugin.

Step 1 — Add JaCoCo to pom.xml
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
    <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>0.8.13</version>

    <executions>
        <execution>
            <goals>
                <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>

        <execution>
            <id>report</id>
            <phase>test</phase>
            <goals>
                <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>

Put this inside:

<build>
    <plugins>
        <!-- JaCoCo here -->
    </plugins>
</build>
Step 2 — Run your tests

From IntelliJ terminal:

mvn clean test

Your TestNG tests execute normally.

JaCoCo watches them in the background.

Step 3 — Open the report

After the build:

target/
   site/
      jacoco/
         index.html

Open:

target/site/jacoco/index.html

You'll get something like:

Package       Class          Line    Branch
------------------------------------------------
service       UserService    85%     75%
repository    UserRepo       92%     80%

Then click UserService and JaCoCo shows which lines/branches were covered.